\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{The proposed edge-direction operator}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Use the rank-#{64} cochain module of \ref{fdip-00YP} over
#{k=\mathbb Z} or #{\mathbb Q}, now with all rotational links
#{R_i(x)=1}. Let #{a_i(x)\in k^2} be arbitrary translation
labels. On coefficients set
#{(T_i f)_I(x)=f_I(x+\varepsilon_i)}, the trivial-link shift, and
#{(M_i f)_I(x)=c(a_i(x))f_I(x)}. The proposed
\vocab{edge-direction operator} is
##{\begin{aligned}
\edgeop_a^+&=\sum_{i=1}^2(u_i\wedge)M_i(T_i-\bbI),\\
\edgeop_a^-&=\sum_{i=1}^2(u_i\cdot)(T_i^{-1}-\bbI)M_i,\\
\edgeop_a&=\edgeop_a^+ +\edgeop_a^-.
\end{aligned}}
The order in the second line is part of the formula. The adjoint
identity for the counting pairing is proved in \ref{fdip-00YX}.
More explicitly, if #{I=\{i_1<\cdots<i_p\}}, then
##{(\edgeop_a^+f)_I(x)=\sum_{j=1}^p(-1)^{j-1}c(a_{i_j}(x))
 \bigl(f_{I\setminus\{i_j\}}(x+\varepsilon_{i_j})
       -f_{I\setminus\{i_j\}}(x)\bigr).}
The operators #{\edgeop_a^+} and #{\edgeop_a^-} raise and lower
cubical degree. The internal Clifford direction acts on the other
exterior factor. This is a separate proposal from
the \vocabk{rotation-only matter operator}{fdip-00YO} #{\extD_R}.}
